WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.129
- When was the first time that you went,

00:00:02.129 --> 00:00:04.170
Had you been to Hawaii before Barack?

00:00:04.170 --> 00:00:05.880
- I had never been to Hawaii.

00:00:05.880 --> 00:00:08.730
So the first summer we started dating-

00:00:08.730 --> 00:00:10.161
- Putting on the moves,
taking you to Hawaii.

00:00:10.161 --> 00:00:11.231
- I know, I know.

00:00:11.231 --> 00:00:13.860
It got serious by fall,

00:00:13.860 --> 00:00:15.930
and he wanted me to come
home and meet his family.

00:00:15.930 --> 00:00:17.970
- Yeah.
- And I was like, "Great."

00:00:17.970 --> 00:00:19.560
'Cause I was in Chicago.

00:00:19.560 --> 00:00:22.260
Chicago is, ooh, dark, gray, cold.

00:00:22.260 --> 00:00:25.980
I was working as an
associate, so I was dog tired.

00:00:25.980 --> 00:00:28.350
So I thought, "Ooh, a romantic getaway

00:00:28.350 --> 00:00:30.439
with my new beau to Hawaii."

00:00:30.439 --> 00:00:31.272
(Kelly laughs)

00:00:31.272 --> 00:00:33.300
And so we go, but I didn't realize

00:00:33.300 --> 00:00:35.160
that it was a family trip.

00:00:35.160 --> 00:00:36.780
I mean, his grandparents were there,

00:00:36.780 --> 00:00:38.940
his mom was there, his little sister.

00:00:38.940 --> 00:00:40.560
I'm thinking-
- That can be intimidating.

00:00:40.560 --> 00:00:43.830
- Well, I'm thinking the only
thing I knew about Hawaii

00:00:43.830 --> 00:00:46.500
was "The Brady Bunch" when they-

00:00:46.500 --> 00:00:47.370
- [Kelly] I didn't even know that.

00:00:47.370 --> 00:00:48.390
I just...
- You didn't?

00:00:48.390 --> 00:00:49.890
I forget. Generationally...

00:00:49.890 --> 00:00:50.730
- No, no.

00:00:50.730 --> 00:00:52.950
I know of the, I didn't
watch it as religiously,

00:00:52.950 --> 00:00:54.300
because they were reruns by then.

00:00:54.300 --> 00:00:55.133
I didn't know that,

00:00:55.133 --> 00:00:56.400
but I just knew the necklace.

00:00:56.400 --> 00:00:57.630
- Yeah. The leis.
- The leis, yeah.

00:00:57.630 --> 00:01:00.330
- The Hawaiian shirts, that was
"The Brady Bunch" in Hawaii.

00:01:00.330 --> 00:01:01.163
- [Kelly] Okay.

00:01:01.163 --> 00:01:02.310
- And so I'm expecting,

00:01:02.310 --> 00:01:03.480
- I love that that's what you knew.

00:01:03.480 --> 00:01:06.394
- Tiki torches and Waikiki

00:01:06.394 --> 00:01:10.080
and mai tais and sunset walks.

00:01:10.080 --> 00:01:12.540
We got there, and it was a family visit.

00:01:12.540 --> 00:01:14.580
We were in his grandparents' apartment,

00:01:14.580 --> 00:01:16.143
which wasn't near the beach.

00:01:17.580 --> 00:01:19.057
We were doing jigsaw puzzles.

00:01:19.057 --> 00:01:20.490
"60 Minutes" was on,

00:01:20.490 --> 00:01:22.380
just like at my grandparents' house.

00:01:22.380 --> 00:01:23.213
- Yeah.
- We were...

00:01:23.213 --> 00:01:24.240
- You could've been anywhere.

00:01:24.240 --> 00:01:26.820
- We were eating tuna fish on TV trays.

00:01:26.820 --> 00:01:28.207
- Another one with tuna fish.

00:01:28.207 --> 00:01:29.700
Look, it's a quick meal.

00:01:29.700 --> 00:01:31.680
- With sweet pickles.

00:01:31.680 --> 00:01:32.787
- Yes.
- Yeah, yeah.

00:01:32.787 --> 00:01:33.620
- The pickles's a little sweet, yeah.

00:01:33.620 --> 00:01:35.190
- So it was a lovely visit.

00:01:35.190 --> 00:01:39.600
But in that youthful
romance I was disappointed

00:01:39.600 --> 00:01:43.260
because I thought we were
just gonna be he and I

00:01:43.260 --> 00:01:45.420
and I tell this story

00:01:45.420 --> 00:01:48.120
because as I tell young
people looking for love,

00:01:48.120 --> 00:01:52.380
you think you're looking for
mai ties in sunset beaches.

00:01:52.380 --> 00:01:54.120
But what you really want is a guy

00:01:54.120 --> 00:01:56.070
that's gonna show up for his family.

00:01:56.070 --> 00:01:56.903
- [Kelly] 100%.

00:01:56.903 --> 00:01:58.080
- And that's what he was doing.

00:01:58.080 --> 00:01:59.073
That's what he was showing me.

00:01:59.073 --> 00:02:01.620
- Yeah.
- On that first visit.

00:02:01.620 --> 00:02:02.453
- You got lucky there.

00:02:02.453 --> 00:02:04.470
So wait, y'all just,
is it 30th anniversary?

00:02:04.470 --> 00:02:05.303
Is that what,

00:02:05.303 --> 00:02:07.002
- 30 years October 3rd.
(audience cheering)

00:02:07.002 --> 00:02:08.818
Woo, woo woo.

00:02:08.818 --> 00:02:10.920
- What did you do to celebrate?

00:02:10.920 --> 00:02:12.843
And look how great y'all look there.

00:02:14.130 --> 00:02:15.554
You got your beach moment.

00:02:15.554 --> 00:02:16.387
- We got,

00:02:16.387 --> 00:02:19.020
There we did, we got a walk on the beach

00:02:19.020 --> 00:02:21.313
but we recreated our honeymoon.

00:02:21.313 --> 00:02:23.550
- [Kelly] Oh.
- And our honeymoon,

00:02:23.550 --> 00:02:26.760
I didn't wanna go anywhere crazy.

00:02:26.760 --> 00:02:29.190
We couldn't afford it, number one.

00:02:29.190 --> 00:02:32.220
So we rented a car,
went to the West Coast,

00:02:32.220 --> 00:02:36.030
started in San Francisco and
just drove along the coast.

00:02:36.030 --> 00:02:38.280
And stopped in really nice places.

00:02:38.280 --> 00:02:39.113
- Ah, I love that.

00:02:39.113 --> 00:02:39.946
- That's what we did in October.

00:02:39.946 --> 00:02:42.360
He recreated all of that.

00:02:42.360 --> 00:02:45.450
Now we were doing it with a
motorcade and Secret Service.

00:02:45.450 --> 00:02:47.640
- Yeah, slightly different.
- It was a little different

00:02:47.640 --> 00:02:49.710
but it was the thought that counted.

00:02:49.710 --> 00:02:51.570
I mean, in that picture
you see on the beach

00:02:51.570 --> 00:02:53.372
there's like 12 guys in the back.

00:02:53.372 --> 00:02:54.205
- [Kelly] That you don't see.

00:02:54.205 --> 00:02:55.800
- Right, we're just,
they're trying to hide

00:02:55.800 --> 00:02:58.920
but it's like we see you behind the rock.

00:02:58.920 --> 00:03:00.120
- Is that annoying?

00:03:00.120 --> 00:03:01.530
Like, that would be hard.

00:03:01.530 --> 00:03:03.630
I mean for all anybody in the public eye

00:03:03.630 --> 00:03:04.860
you get photos taken everywhere.

00:03:04.860 --> 00:03:07.620
But just to have people around
all the time, that's hard.

00:03:07.620 --> 00:03:08.970
- You get adjusted to it.

00:03:08.970 --> 00:03:11.340
And our secrets, the Secret Service agents

00:03:11.340 --> 00:03:12.560
they are like our family.

00:03:12.560 --> 00:03:13.393
- [Kelly] All right.

00:03:13.393 --> 00:03:14.226
And so,

00:03:14.226 --> 00:03:15.773
- But I love my family, but
I also like being alone.

00:03:15.773 --> 00:03:17.289
- [Michelle] I know. I know.

00:03:17.289 --> 00:03:18.210
- [Kelly] Yeah.
- You know?

00:03:18.210 --> 00:03:20.760
- I always tell, and I
had to tell the girls this

00:03:20.760 --> 00:03:22.680
that throughout the White House, it's like

00:03:22.680 --> 00:03:24.030
you don't complain about this.

00:03:24.030 --> 00:03:25.890
This is what service is.

00:03:25.890 --> 00:03:27.180
This is part of it.

00:03:27.180 --> 00:03:28.650
It was an honor to serve

00:03:28.650 --> 00:03:31.620
and you deal, there's
shortcomings that come with it.

00:03:31.620 --> 00:03:33.780
- They're sacrifices.
- They're sacrifices.

00:03:33.780 --> 00:03:35.640
But I'm not gonna complain

00:03:35.640 --> 00:03:38.130
when people are serving in the military

00:03:38.130 --> 00:03:39.600
and they have real hardships

00:03:39.600 --> 00:03:42.480
and their military families and
families who are struggling.

00:03:42.480 --> 00:03:44.700
I am not gonna complain
about Secret Service.

00:03:44.700 --> 00:03:45.533
- Absolutely not.

00:03:45.533 --> 00:03:47.430
But if you just wanna like,
make out a little on the beach,

00:03:47.430 --> 00:03:48.750
I don't want someone watching me.

00:03:48.750 --> 00:03:49.900
- Well, it's true.

00:03:49.900 --> 00:03:52.200
They, they tend to turn
away, they look way.

00:03:52.200 --> 00:03:54.570
- If they're not, change those people out.

00:03:54.570 --> 00:03:55.440
- That's right.

00:03:55.440 --> 00:03:56.760
- So speaking of looking great,

00:03:56.760 --> 00:03:57.593
y'all look so great in that.

00:03:57.593 --> 00:03:59.580
So on your recent press tour, you said

00:03:59.580 --> 00:04:02.640
that you didn't feel comfortable
wearing natural hairstyles

00:04:02.640 --> 00:04:03.473
while in the White House.

00:04:03.473 --> 00:04:05.220
So what exactly do you mean by that?

00:04:05.220 --> 00:04:07.380
- You know, a lot of people don't remember

00:04:07.380 --> 00:04:10.710
as the first black family
in the White House,

00:04:10.710 --> 00:04:13.500
the journey there wasn't smooth.

00:04:13.500 --> 00:04:15.270
There were people who tried to,

00:04:15.270 --> 00:04:16.170
- I remember the news, yeah.

00:04:16.170 --> 00:04:18.450
- Turn us into others.

00:04:18.450 --> 00:04:19.283
- Yeah. Division.

00:04:19.283 --> 00:04:22.170
- And so we had to overcome a lot of that.

00:04:22.170 --> 00:04:26.700
And so much was read into
gestures we would make

00:04:26.700 --> 00:04:28.350
like a fist bump was turned

00:04:28.350 --> 00:04:31.440
into a terrorist fist jab.
- What?

00:04:31.440 --> 00:04:34.020
- And so I was like, braids,

00:04:34.020 --> 00:04:36.960
people aren't gonna
understand braids, sadly.

00:04:36.960 --> 00:04:38.520
Not at least at that time.

00:04:38.520 --> 00:04:41.460
And I didn't want that
to be a distraction.

00:04:41.460 --> 00:04:43.320
There was too much to do.

00:04:43.320 --> 00:04:46.590
There was healthcare to (cross talking)

00:04:46.590 --> 00:04:50.130
Now, fortunately, you
know, the more people

00:04:50.130 --> 00:04:52.320
are showing what it means to be beautiful,

00:04:52.320 --> 00:04:53.580
what hair can look like.

00:04:53.580 --> 00:04:55.440
I'm grateful to this generation

00:04:55.440 --> 00:04:57.660
that is owning every part of themselves.

00:04:57.660 --> 00:04:58.493
- Absolutely.

00:04:58.493 --> 00:04:59.400
- So.

00:04:59.400 --> 00:05:01.187
- Now we're a bold
generation and it's good.

00:05:01.187 --> 00:05:02.400
- And we need it.

00:05:02.400 --> 00:05:05.040
- Beause we need to create
a broader definition

00:05:05.040 --> 00:05:08.460
of who's American, who
counts, what beauty is.

00:05:08.460 --> 00:05:10.650
It only helps our kids who are,

00:05:10.650 --> 00:05:12.780
we don't know who they're
gonna become, right?

00:05:12.780 --> 00:05:14.880
And we wanna make sure
there's a space for them

00:05:14.880 --> 00:05:15.713
whoever they choose to be.

00:05:15.713 --> 00:05:16.830
- [Kelly] Why limit them?

00:05:16.830 --> 00:05:19.050
Yeah. Like, I think is,
it should be celebrated.

00:05:19.050 --> 00:05:19.883
And I think you're right.

00:05:19.883 --> 00:05:21.510
This younger generation is
very much into that, yeah.

00:05:21.510 --> 00:05:23.460
- And it shouldn't be politicized.

00:05:23.460 --> 00:05:24.293
- Yeah.

00:05:24.293 --> 00:05:26.580
- Most kids who are wearing
tattoos and piercings,

00:05:26.580 --> 00:05:27.840
they've got long nails,

00:05:27.840 --> 00:05:32.840
it's not about, their value
system is about individuality.

00:05:33.060 --> 00:05:35.100
- But back in the day, like
tattoos was like so bad.

00:05:35.100 --> 00:05:36.120
- It, well, and

00:05:36.120 --> 00:05:37.320
- And I came home with one

00:05:37.320 --> 00:05:39.270
and my mom just kind of looked
at it was like, that's new.

00:05:39.270 --> 00:05:40.477
- Well it was the same thing.

00:05:40.477 --> 00:05:43.197
My generation didn't, we didn't,

00:05:43.197 --> 00:05:45.540
tattoos meant something totally different.

00:05:45.540 --> 00:05:46.890
- [Kelly] I know.
- We used to threaten our kids

00:05:46.890 --> 00:05:48.390
that if you get a tattoo,

00:05:48.390 --> 00:05:50.460
we're gonna get exactly the same kind

00:05:50.460 --> 00:05:52.710
and show it on TikTok or whatever.

00:05:52.710 --> 00:05:53.543
- Take the cool away.

00:05:53.543 --> 00:05:55.375
- Take the cool all away.

00:05:55.375 --> 00:05:57.428
- That's a good parenting skill.

00:05:57.428 --> 00:06:00.720
- Just like we're gonna make it so uncool.

00:06:00.720 --> 00:06:03.363
Barack Obama's gonna have
a heart on his shoulders.

00:06:04.950 --> 00:06:05.783
It's like...

00:06:05.783 --> 00:06:09.210
- That's the greatest mom tip of the day.

00:06:09.210 --> 00:06:11.580
We have another question
from another rad mama viewer.

00:06:11.580 --> 00:06:13.447
So Tracy from New York asked,

00:06:13.447 --> 00:06:15.210
"I was raised by a single mother

00:06:15.210 --> 00:06:16.890
and worked all through high school.

00:06:16.890 --> 00:06:18.210
I paid for my own college.

00:06:18.210 --> 00:06:20.730
And today I'm a successful business owner.

00:06:20.730 --> 00:06:23.670
My daughters are living a
life I could only dream of."

00:06:23.670 --> 00:06:25.174
A lot of us relate to this Tracy.

00:06:25.174 --> 00:06:26.007
- Yep.

00:06:26.007 --> 00:06:28.230
"Even so, I worry about them all the time.

00:06:28.230 --> 00:06:30.090
I worry they're not
learning the tough lessons

00:06:30.090 --> 00:06:31.650
that made me who I am.

00:06:31.650 --> 00:06:33.960
I also worry that the
world they're growing up in

00:06:33.960 --> 00:06:35.790
is just too different from mine.

00:06:35.790 --> 00:06:37.560
What advice can you give me?"

00:06:37.560 --> 00:06:38.393
That's a loaded question.

00:06:38.393 --> 00:06:39.540
- Yeah, yeah, yeah.

00:06:39.540 --> 00:06:41.730
- I think all of us feel
that way 'cause we want,

00:06:41.730 --> 00:06:44.520
you want each, you want to do better,

00:06:44.520 --> 00:06:45.690
your family to do better, but it's,

00:06:45.690 --> 00:06:46.920
we all worry about that.
- And I don't know about you,

00:06:46.920 --> 00:06:50.507
it's like my life is so different

00:06:50.507 --> 00:06:52.410
from how I grew up.

00:06:52.410 --> 00:06:54.270
I mean, there wasn't the plan to

00:06:54.270 --> 00:06:56.040
live in the White House, to be touring.

00:06:56.040 --> 00:06:56.910
I mean that wasn't the,

00:06:56.910 --> 00:06:58.200
- That wasn't on your to-do list.

00:06:58.200 --> 00:07:00.600
- Those weren't the the
people we came from.

00:07:00.600 --> 00:07:01.470
- Right.
- Yeah.

00:07:01.470 --> 00:07:03.690
- So, but I loved my upbringing.

00:07:03.690 --> 00:07:05.160
I loved the hard knocks.

00:07:05.160 --> 00:07:06.210
I love the,
- It made me who I am.

00:07:06.210 --> 00:07:07.043
- Exactly.

00:07:07.043 --> 00:07:08.430
So I definitely get that

00:07:08.430 --> 00:07:10.530
because my kids grew
up in the White House.

00:07:10.530 --> 00:07:12.660
- [Kelly] Yeah.
- They grew up with security.

00:07:12.660 --> 00:07:14.220
- [Kelly] Yeah. So do mine.

00:07:14.220 --> 00:07:15.180
- [Both] That is not normal.

00:07:15.180 --> 00:07:16.400
- That's, exactly.

00:07:16.400 --> 00:07:19.080
So I spent all eight years just trying

00:07:19.080 --> 00:07:20.640
to take stuff away from 'em.

00:07:20.640 --> 00:07:23.280
It's like, don't make their
bed, having housekeeping.

00:07:23.280 --> 00:07:25.080
It's like don't go in their rooms.

00:07:25.080 --> 00:07:26.880
Yeah. They have to know
how to make their beds.

00:07:26.880 --> 00:07:29.010
But so I understand.

00:07:29.010 --> 00:07:30.900
I think we all understand those worries.

00:07:30.900 --> 00:07:32.670
If you have, if you've grown up

00:07:32.670 --> 00:07:35.280
with a certain set of values you want

00:07:35.280 --> 00:07:38.403
and your life is, is noticeably different.

00:07:39.480 --> 00:07:43.170
Here's what I experience is
that kids are watching us.

00:07:43.170 --> 00:07:46.680
They watch what we do,
not what we give them.

00:07:46.680 --> 00:07:49.860
And we can't be so afraid of the world

00:07:49.860 --> 00:07:51.270
that they're gonna enter into.

00:07:51.270 --> 00:07:52.785
Because it may be different for us

00:07:52.785 --> 00:07:55.500
but it won't be for them.

00:07:55.500 --> 00:07:58.323
Those are our fears,
once again, taking over.

00:07:59.280 --> 00:08:02.853
I found that if you give your kids love,

00:08:02.853 --> 00:08:05.520
if they feel seen by you,

00:08:05.520 --> 00:08:08.850
if you don't parent from guilt,

00:08:08.850 --> 00:08:11.700
if you give them boundaries and discipline

00:08:11.700 --> 00:08:13.440
it doesn't matter whether they grow up

00:08:13.440 --> 00:08:15.420
in the White House or whether they grow up

00:08:15.420 --> 00:08:18.810
in the little bitty apartment
I did on 74th and Euclid,

00:08:18.810 --> 00:08:23.310
my girls have been watching
their, me and Barack,

00:08:23.310 --> 00:08:24.840
how we operate in the world.

00:08:24.840 --> 00:08:27.030
How we treat other people.

00:08:27.030 --> 00:08:28.980
They have had way more than we do.

00:08:28.980 --> 00:08:30.540
But they know right and wrong.

00:08:30.540 --> 00:08:32.160
They know what we care about.

00:08:32.160 --> 00:08:35.970
And I would just say, don't
worry about who you've become.

00:08:35.970 --> 00:08:38.310
If you're doing those
things for your kids,

00:08:38.310 --> 00:08:39.660
they're gonna be okay.

00:08:39.660 --> 00:08:41.160
They may slip,

00:08:41.160 --> 00:08:44.070
they may do things that you
need to pull 'em back on

00:08:44.070 --> 00:08:46.770
but you gotta see who they're gonna become

00:08:46.770 --> 00:08:49.560
and be there to advise them as they go.

00:08:49.560 --> 00:08:50.393
- [Kelly] Yeah.

00:08:50.393 --> 00:08:51.840
- I think that's all we can do.

00:08:51.840 --> 00:08:55.980
- I think also like coming
back to the small things.

00:08:55.980 --> 00:08:58.440
Like I, my kids are doing laundry.

00:08:58.440 --> 00:08:59.273
They're six and eight,

00:08:59.273 --> 00:09:00.770
they're doing laundry and
they're taking out trash for me.

00:09:00.770 --> 00:09:02.760
- My kids did that the same thing.

00:09:02.760 --> 00:09:03.810
They had to clean their rooms.

00:09:03.810 --> 00:09:04.800
They had to,
- Those small things.

00:09:04.800 --> 00:09:06.540
Like to make them be accountable or like,

00:09:06.540 --> 00:09:08.280
- They had jobs.
- Yeah.

00:09:08.280 --> 00:09:09.630
- Each of them had to have a job

00:09:09.630 --> 00:09:10.840
didn't need a job.

00:09:10.840 --> 00:09:12.093
- [Kelly] Yeah.
- But they had to have a job.

00:09:12.093 --> 00:09:12.926
- But that's important.

00:09:12.926 --> 00:09:14.647
Like, I needed the jobs,
like whenever I had them.

00:09:14.647 --> 00:09:15.990
- So did I.
- Yeah.

00:09:15.990 --> 00:09:17.670
But like for them, I'm like, you want

00:09:17.670 --> 00:09:20.850
'cause I talked to my girl,
she's I swear she's going on 20,

00:09:20.850 --> 00:09:23.460
she's eight, but like, but
she's like, already wants a job.

